You’re almost there – the source definition itself is fine, but a classic OSM raster tile service uses **256 px tiles**, while MapLibre’s raster source defaults to **512 px**. If you leave the default `tileSize` unchanged the map will appear at the wrong zoom level (tiles look “zoomed out” and labels can be mis-aligned) and you’ll get a blank or mis-scaled raster.

Add the `tileSize: 256` property (and, optionally, the usual metadata) and then add a raster layer that points at the source:

```js
// 1️⃣ Define the raster source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: [
    'https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'
  ],
  tileSize: 256,          // <-- required for OSM tiles
  minzoom: 0,             // optional, but makes the range explicit
  maxzoom: 19,            // OSM serves up to z=19
  attribution: '&copy; <a href="https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright">OpenStreetMap</a> contributors'
});

// 2️⃣ Add a raster layer that uses the source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-basemap',
  // Insert it below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels
  // (find the first symbol layer id programmatically if you want it robust)
  // e.g. map.addLayer(..., firstSymbolLayerId);
});
```

**What this does**

| Piece | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `tileSize: 256` | OSM tiles are 256 px; without it MapLibre assumes 512 px and the imagery appears at the wrong effective zoom. |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Gives MapLibre the correct zoom range; otherwise it will request zoom levels the server doesn’t provide (you’ll see empty tiles when you zoom past the source’s limits). |
| `attribution` | Required by OSM’s license and shows up in the map’s attribution control. |
| Layer order (insert below first `symbol` layer) | Prevents your raster from covering street-name labels and other symbols that are usually drawn on top. |

With those additions the OSM raster tiles will load and render correctly. No extra CORS handling is needed—`tile.openstreetmap.org` already sends the proper `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header.

